[{:db/id "q-342263/field-1/65dc7d6f0ee23b956c60b5c7d00b706df142a178220ed2c0868c605a078f9076" :answer/id #uuid "e1f493e9-ac43-5e90-ad09-d53345fad8e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{e^{-2s}}{(s-1)(s+1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342263/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "7e32bb26-f558-5832-98c6-556ade47a471" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228627 :answer-field/correct "q-342263/field-1/65dc7d6f0ee23b956c60b5c7d00b706df142a178220ed2c0868c605a078f9076" :answer-field/choices ["q-342263/field-1/65dc7d6f0ee23b956c60b5c7d00b706df142a178220ed2c0868c605a078f9076" "q-342263/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-342263"] :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y={u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>1$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+y} & =L{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =L{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)={e}^{t-2}.$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed exponential forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-2)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{{e}^{(t+2)-2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =L{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}} \\\\ sY(s)+Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s-1)(s+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-342772/field-1/095857fba7f3d9be1ab88c07fcecef2725e27f11d9e8ccf5b6eecc00072f0a2b" :answer/id #uuid "6e174c9d-78a9-596b-a1fa-19bf3f9e719b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5se^{-\\pi s/4}}{(s^2+9)(s+2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342772/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6079d035-404c-5ede-914b-fd82afd6a088" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342772/field-1/095857fba7f3d9be1ab88c07fcecef2725e27f11d9e8ccf5b6eecc00072f0a2b" :answer-field/choices ["q-342772/field-1/095857fba7f3d9be1ab88c07fcecef2725e27f11d9e8ccf5b6eecc00072f0a2b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342772" :question/id #uuid "19251858-5a30-58a4-afb5-b93b4748ad80" :question/math-academy-id "q-342772" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\,,\\,y(0)=-3,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /4}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=-\\frac{3}{s+2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+2y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+2L{y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-3:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-(-3)=sY(s)+3\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $f(t)=5cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing.\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{5u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /4)s}\\cdot L{5cos\\,(3\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/4}\\cdot L{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/4}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+2L{y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} \\\\ (sY(s)+3)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s)+3 & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =-3+\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{3}{s+2}+\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-342772/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342772"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342532/field-1/1eac1425d9fe2c015636c541468750179b38f715806cd1c9903937578cd3f2d6" :answer/id #uuid "6da78bf4-4c9d-5737-84b4-251cf68ae268" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{12e^{-2s}}{s^4(s+4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342532/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "d5d0051d-e47c-5864-b1fb-3c2b3c965398" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342532/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e93bdb3-8302-5b3d-a9dd-a5e800bd2a8e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342532/field-1/1eac1425d9fe2c015636c541468750179b38f715806cd1c9903937578cd3f2d6" :answer-field/choices ["q-342532/field-1/1eac1425d9fe2c015636c541468750179b38f715806cd1c9903937578cd3f2d6" "q-342532/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342532" :question/id #uuid "cc2cc105-4de0-5e6d-86ad-16217f6f33c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-342532" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+4y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+4L{y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)=2(t-2)^{3}.$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{2u(t-2)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{2((t+2)-2)^{3}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{2{t}^{3}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{3!}{{s}^{3+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+4L{y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)(t-2)^{3}} \\\\ sY(s)+4Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-342532/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342532"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342540/field-1/4435ab6b3bc7da78254cba57e45d91a61caed721613103ac8b2de20f60760662" :answer/id #uuid "aac89823-758a-5502-ac15-8284dad88e7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2e^{-2s}}{(s-4)(s-2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342540/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "012e1b76-1ce1-58a6-a2a8-a616f2be0488" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342540/field-1/4435ab6b3bc7da78254cba57e45d91a61caed721613103ac8b2de20f60760662" :answer-field/choices ["q-342540/field-1/4435ab6b3bc7da78254cba57e45d91a61caed721613103ac8b2de20f60760662"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342540" :question/id #uuid "a5f2f9aa-2bba-58b3-96ef-f66971706fb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-342540" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{4t-8}\\,,\\,y(0)=-1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=-\\frac{1}{s-2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>4$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}-2y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{4t-8}} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}-2L{y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{4t-8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=1:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-(-1)=sY(s)+1\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{4t-8}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)=2{e}^{4t-8}.$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{2u(t-2)\\cdot {e}^{4t-8}} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{2{e}^{4(t+2)-8}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{2{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{s-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}-2L{y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t){e}^{4t-8}} \\\\ (sY(s)+1)-2Y(s) & =\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s-4} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s)+1 & =\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s-4} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =-1+\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s-4} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{1}{s-2}+\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{(s-4)(s-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342540/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342540"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342267/field-1/eee0bf67aecb0481decd8c91d98f47516b44c2d881daefd2a0f03ec28c0e8c30" :answer/id #uuid "4b90809c-1210-5518-9584-91143b4b4e13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{e^{-\\pi s}}{(s^2+1)(s+1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342267/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5afb84ea-36fc-50a5-acc4-4aacb6f9a41a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342267/field-1/eee0bf67aecb0481decd8c91d98f47516b44c2d881daefd2a0f03ec28c0e8c30" :answer-field/choices ["q-342267/field-1/eee0bf67aecb0481decd8c91d98f47516b44c2d881daefd2a0f03ec28c0e8c30"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342267" :question/id #uuid "07c536b1-829e-5b30-a52d-190eedb4d251" :question/math-academy-id "q-342267" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y={u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi }(t)=u(t-\\pi )$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+y} & =L{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =L{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\pi $ and $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi ).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-\\pi )\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )} & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡((t+\\pi )-\\pi )} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =L{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )} \\\\ sY(s)+Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{({s}^{2}+1)(s+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-342267/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342267"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342812/selection/cad0783b70686773b946d3cc9247ca14eebf96d05336b9da7a9fb6fcc3c00e40" :answer/id #uuid "7663da89-f311-55ae-9e07-4b4b7a64b121" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-3t}-{e}^{-4t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342812/selection/2cfced63c75316a05c3758cf965eee0966e3ad2a9905e671cc71a6786aa066f1" :answer/id #uuid "536e7b22-8c9e-5bdf-9537-f997f58238ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342812/selection/16c4f7c5ed830ea5fa5ba1a653a6c40bfdd13e5b00efa87e70e5627d61163af8" :answer/id #uuid "dc72c1f2-59bb-5e47-8fd6-b86ab748e721" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342812/selection/6dab18d102456199d068255704f205c0c59d70020ad5fe6c86bd8789fce83099" :answer/id #uuid "5bfe6e95-4685-5926-9861-406ad22316f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-3(t-2)}+{e}^{-4(t-2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342812/selection/b1cfb7612748c5b46aa00d80d729397f91a6ea823ea8fa3347f1ca61eb59ecb1" :answer/id #uuid "78a5d5bb-002a-57fc-8a42-4fbe6bab13cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{e}^{-3(t-2)}+{e}^{-4(t-2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342812/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3dcde624-71cd-59bf-920c-9d41b88bb279" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-342812/selection/2cfced63c75316a05c3758cf965eee0966e3ad2a9905e671cc71a6786aa066f1" :answer-field/choices ["q-342812/selection/cad0783b70686773b946d3cc9247ca14eebf96d05336b9da7a9fb6fcc3c00e40" "q-342812/selection/2cfced63c75316a05c3758cf965eee0966e3ad2a9905e671cc71a6786aa066f1" "q-342812/selection/16c4f7c5ed830ea5fa5ba1a653a6c40bfdd13e5b00efa87e70e5627d61163af8" "q-342812/selection/6dab18d102456199d068255704f205c0c59d70020ad5fe6c86bd8789fce83099" "q-342812/selection/b1cfb7612748c5b46aa00d80d729397f91a6ea823ea8fa3347f1ca61eb59ecb1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342812" :question/id #uuid "53cee26f-218b-5a31-8775-1bbe128e9dcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-342812" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y={u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{-4(t-2)}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s+3)(s+4)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=u(t-2)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the initial value problem, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s).$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first ignore the exponential term ${e}^{-2s}$ (which corresponds to a time shift) and focus on the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s+3)(s+4)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s+3)(s+4)}=\\frac{A}{s+3}+\\frac{B}{s+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+4)+B(s+3).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(1)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=1.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-1)\\,⟹\\,B=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s+4}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s+4}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3t}-{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-2s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2)f(t-2) & =u(t-2)[{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=u(t-2)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-342812/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f316163e-7e1e-5c7f-a75d-9c917dd8bcda"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342812"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-342314"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y={u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)\\,,\\,y(0)=1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{2}(s+1)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-2s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+1)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+1)$ and a repeated linear factor ${s}^{2},$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+1)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{s+1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=As(s+1)+B(s+1)+C{s}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(1)+C(0)\\,⟹\\,B=1.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(0)+C(1)\\,⟹\\,C=1.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+C\\,⟹\\,A=-C=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{s+1}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{s+1}} \\\\  & =-1+t+{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-2s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2)f(t-2) & =u(t-2)(-1+(t-2)+{e}^{-(t-2)}) \\\\  & =u(t-2)(t-3+{e}^{-(t-2)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)={e}^{-t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-(t-2)}+t-3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-342815/selection/e087d6be10b63b0e0f00d162969ac341e27d7b679a345c015bf52d40f94ed836" :answer/id #uuid "d3efe532-1dca-5d3f-a34b-637bae7bd08b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342815/selection/9a0e069da918e18e3a4eb2fdbb94fd30ef96f3d74b2aaa89859ce71bfe43471c" :answer/id #uuid "59f19a04-eb40-546f-930c-71447fff7494" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342815/selection/50226370c5157f38754a9a82a4dbb210a383733a8acfd3992c2480d62de2368b" :answer/id #uuid "50ec8ab1-0bc1-53c1-9ff8-15b21d2bd25b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342815/selection/33ca01cc1c8dd04d1ce81ac1e2712eb3a87135c1600768dd4262e61a0ac8e56c" :answer/id #uuid "e60db8f9-294f-5ee0-babd-abd38f553b16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342815/selection/7b46ea4d3619e479f5ceab82e0ca865dfb46eaa7fe767a963d9a7596594a532a" :answer/id #uuid "eacd7eee-bebd-55c7-b760-dc1cc951bac6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342815/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1d576752-3a6d-500c-bf5d-2bfe15a5a24f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-342815/selection/50226370c5157f38754a9a82a4dbb210a383733a8acfd3992c2480d62de2368b" :answer-field/choices ["q-342815/selection/e087d6be10b63b0e0f00d162969ac341e27d7b679a345c015bf52d40f94ed836" "q-342815/selection/9a0e069da918e18e3a4eb2fdbb94fd30ef96f3d74b2aaa89859ce71bfe43471c" "q-342815/selection/50226370c5157f38754a9a82a4dbb210a383733a8acfd3992c2480d62de2368b" "q-342815/selection/33ca01cc1c8dd04d1ce81ac1e2712eb3a87135c1600768dd4262e61a0ac8e56c" "q-342815/selection/7b46ea4d3619e479f5ceab82e0ca865dfb46eaa7fe767a963d9a7596594a532a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342815" :question/id #uuid "2c77a8de-9c5b-51fc-9f97-106b8202c181" :question/math-academy-id "q-342815" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y={u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /2}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+2}+\\frac{s\\,{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{4}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$ \n\nHint: You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+4),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns=A({s}^{2}+4)+(Bs+C)(s+2).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n-2=A((-2)^{2}+4)+0\\,⟹\\,8A=-2\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n0=4A+2C\\,⟹\\,0=4\\,(-\\frac{1}{4})+2C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{4}(-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-{e}^{-2t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $cos\\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ and $sin\\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})({e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{4}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-342815/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f316163e-7e1e-5c7f-a75d-9c917dd8bcda"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342815"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342780/selection/b9522627ea454f8beaf5dd2220b8097f53b788d2b3f7db696f8101ce51363437" :answer/id #uuid "6e09a721-1036-5837-834d-c98cde8a3615" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}-\\frac{3t-10}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342780/selection/ae1563ff3a2ac2e6af8587b4f85928aba616e486c20a6e723eba24a46e36c621" :answer/id #uuid "99676ecf-9c6f-5dc3-9851-56c3efc0f562" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{10-3t}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342780/selection/6f60e27b3f2a0e86c47c90bf6273cbdb9b30cb044e0e96c4734a893227f1e7ee" :answer/id #uuid "d02dc950-9492-56ae-879d-ab62b4b49db3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342780/selection/8bda147ffdeae1cb8db84fe25b45832827eeb4ae4a30506c8ff747bb4ced4989" :answer/id #uuid "845a76c6-a804-5913-ba29-e237814455ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-8}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342780/selection/860b94c06aae503c9f215cf09f17e0ef7288eec3a1f0f381a86b1eff79564b25" :answer/id #uuid "116cfe15-a57a-5ec7-b642-b16b342d211a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342780/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d8996ccf-868a-54ec-a333-1d72a7be3fac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-342780/selection/860b94c06aae503c9f215cf09f17e0ef7288eec3a1f0f381a86b1eff79564b25" :answer-field/choices ["q-342780/selection/b9522627ea454f8beaf5dd2220b8097f53b788d2b3f7db696f8101ce51363437" "q-342780/selection/ae1563ff3a2ac2e6af8587b4f85928aba616e486c20a6e723eba24a46e36c621" "q-342780/selection/6f60e27b3f2a0e86c47c90bf6273cbdb9b30cb044e0e96c4734a893227f1e7ee" "q-342780/selection/8bda147ffdeae1cb8db84fe25b45832827eeb4ae4a30506c8ff747bb4ced4989" "q-342780/selection/860b94c06aae503c9f215cf09f17e0ef7288eec3a1f0f381a86b1eff79564b25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342780" :question/id #uuid "08e457fa-a4a3-5756-a919-287afdc4b3f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-342780" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3y={u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+3)$ and a repeated linear factor ${s}^{2},$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{s+3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=As(s+3)+B(s+3)+C{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(3)+C(0)\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(0)+C(9)\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+C\\,⟹\\,A=-C=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)(-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t-3}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}) \\\\  & =u(t-3)(\\frac{3t-10}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342780/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f316163e-7e1e-5c7f-a75d-9c917dd8bcda"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342780"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342814/selection/e5b8879d69d3cdc83171623a624c6907e6a95e77fefac11acc597f54c83bbbeb" :answer/id #uuid "844edddf-5f86-5ce3-8691-646be4e9847f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342814/selection/71ef1a3b6c2137655c4e6a5b03f128077e97ab604f1303fb4a44f70f6e3ffc80" :answer/id #uuid "693a09c7-e34d-5ef2-b934-55fd11c07227" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{e}^{(t-3)}+{e}^{-2(t-3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342814/selection/f45e5df9640d04d52b3d384921ef304444606fb6a6835f5e5301915277c67a81" :answer/id #uuid "c96b21c6-31e1-5e79-843a-823bf30e95d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t+3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342814/selection/4cd8d7de79cdc7582a7a10ae48f294edfe823e41e340b2d6d929ca487cf73b30" :answer/id #uuid "5b464c4c-0a40-5787-9c7d-0e887dc37c19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t-3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342814/selection/997a9c58a45f24d27c936631183e515896507c32b7717f3048482bb176fd2fa9" :answer/id #uuid "75297f05-2ad8-5e88-a87e-59e046a0e85c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{(t-3)}+{e}^{-2(t-3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342814/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ccb1d94-5fc6-55aa-9d1b-7670b9652605" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-342814/selection/4cd8d7de79cdc7582a7a10ae48f294edfe823e41e340b2d6d929ca487cf73b30" :answer-field/choices ["q-342814/selection/e5b8879d69d3cdc83171623a624c6907e6a95e77fefac11acc597f54c83bbbeb" "q-342814/selection/71ef1a3b6c2137655c4e6a5b03f128077e97ab604f1303fb4a44f70f6e3ffc80" "q-342814/selection/f45e5df9640d04d52b3d384921ef304444606fb6a6835f5e5301915277c67a81" "q-342814/selection/4cd8d7de79cdc7582a7a10ae48f294edfe823e41e340b2d6d929ca487cf73b30" "q-342814/selection/997a9c58a45f24d27c936631183e515896507c32b7717f3048482bb176fd2fa9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342814" :question/id #uuid "f3c83e4c-8694-59e1-8798-a401bb49c214" :question/math-academy-id "q-342814" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-y={u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{-2(t-3)}\\,,\\,y(0)=2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{2}{s-1}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{(s-1)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=2{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{3}u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s-1)(s+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s-1)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s-1}+\\frac{B}{s+2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+2)+B(s-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(3)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-3)\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{1}{s+2}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}({L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}}-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =\\frac{1}{3}u(t-3)\\cdot [{e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t-3)}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=2{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{3}u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t-3)}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-342814/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f316163e-7e1e-5c7f-a75d-9c917dd8bcda"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342814"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21860"] :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\ny' - 3y = 2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $u_{\\pi/2}(t)=u\\!\\left(t-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > 0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}-3y} & =L{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(2t-\\pi )} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}-3L{y} & =L{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(2t-\\pi )}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $\\mathcal L\\left\\{y' \\right\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y' \\right\\}  = sY(s) - y(0)   = sY(s) + 2\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $f(t)=2\\sin(2t-\\pi).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\} = e^{-as} \\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\pi )} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L{2sin\\,(2\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\pi )} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}-3L{y} & =L{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\pi )} \\\\ (sY(s)+2)-3Y(s) & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s-3)Y(s)+2 & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s-3)Y(s) & =-2+\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21861"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' + 4y = u_3(t)\\cdot (t-3)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $u_3(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s) = -\\dfrac{2}{s+4} + \\dfrac{e^{-3s}}{s^2(s+4)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t) = -2e^{-4t} + u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential $e^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+4)$ and a repeated linear factor $s^2,$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)} = \\dfrac{A}{s} + \\dfrac{B}{s^2} + \\dfrac{C}{s+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1 = As(s+4) + B(s+4) + Cs^2.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A, B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1 = A(0) + B(4) + C(0) \\implies B = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n1 = A(0) + B(0) + C(16) \\implies C = \\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of $s^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n0 = A + C \\implies A = -C = -\\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s) = -\\dfrac{1}{16s} + \\dfrac{1}{4s^2} + \\dfrac{1}{16(s+4)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t) = \\mathcal L^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{16s}+\\frac{1}{4{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{16(s+4)}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{t}{4}+\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the $e^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\ne^{-as}\\cdot F(s)  = \\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1} \\{ e^{-as}F(s) \\} = u(t-a) \\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)[-\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{t-3}{4}+\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4(t-3)}] \\\\  & =u(t-3)(\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4(t-3)}+\\frac{4t-13}{16}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t) = -2e^{-4t} + u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t) = \\dfrac{1}{16}e^{-4(t-3)} + \\dfrac{4t-13}{16}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
